<Page path="start/concepts">
<section id="words">
  <h2 id="words"><a href="#words">Five words</a></h2>
  <div class="tw"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Word</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td data-l="Word"><strong>Multiverse</strong></td><td data-l="Meaning">The definition, one <code>multiverse.yaml</code>: the repositories it is made of, the apps that run them, and how a copy of it is addressed. A repository can define several, one file each. Kept wherever you like and used from anywhere with <code>-m</code> or <code>-f</code>. A <strong>catalog</strong> is only an index of multiverses; it defines nothing.</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Word"><strong>Universe</strong></td><td data-l="Meaning">One complete copy of that multiverse: a git worktree of every repository, one compose project per app, and its own set of hostnames. Numbered (<code>3</code>) or named after a branch (<code>fix/login</code>).</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Word"><strong>App</strong></td><td data-l="Meaning">A runnable unit inside a universe: one repository's compose files, started as their own compose project. A repository usually has exactly one, and does not have to say so.</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Word"><strong>Host</strong></td><td data-l="Meaning">One address an app answers on, named by a key: host <code>api</code> in universe 3 is <code>api-u3.&lt;domain&gt;</code>. A host knows which service and container port serve it.</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Word"><strong>Entrypoint</strong></td><td data-l="Meaning">A port the shared proxy listens on, named. Every universe shares them, because requests are told apart by hostname rather than by port.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
</section>

<section id="shape">
  <h2 id="shape"><a href="#shape">How they nest</a></h2>
  <Terminal><pre><span class="c">multiverse "acme"            multiverse.yaml</span>
├── universe 3               <span class="c">~/acme/universes/3</span>
│   ├── app api              <span class="c">compose acme-u3-api</span>
│   │   ├── service web      <span class="c">host api  → api-u3.example.test</span>
│   │   └── service db       <span class="c">no host; reachable inside the app</span>
│   └── app web              <span class="c">compose acme-u3-web</span>
│       └── service app-dev  <span class="c">host app  → app-u3.example.test</span>
└── universe fix/login       <span class="c">~/acme/universes/fix-login</span>
    └── ...                  <span class="c">the same shape, its own everything</span></pre></Terminal>
  <p>Each level namespaces the one below: multiverse, universe, app. A container is <code>acme-u3-api-web-1</code>, so two repositories can both call a service <code>app</code> without meeting.</p>
</section>

<section id="not-vm">
  <h2 id="not-vm"><a href="#not-vm">It is not a virtual machine</a></h2>
  <p>A universe is a namespace, not a sandbox. It shares the kernel, the runtime, the image cache and the git object store — which is why a new one costs seconds and the second does not double your disk use — and keeps apart the four things that collide: checkouts, container names, volumes and hostnames.</p>
  <p>So it is not isolated for security: universes share your daemon and your user, so anything in one can start, stop or read the containers of another through the runtime, and reach any address the proxy serves. And it is not reproducible the way an image is — it is your machine, with your daemon and your caches.</p>
</section>
</Page>
